Constraints:
	
	Prevents inconsistent or incorrect data. Negative salary, invalid department ID
	Constrains restrict data entry(insert, updated, delete) and prevent invalid entries
	Constraint prevent dropping tables
	Constraint ensure data consistency and data integrity in the DB
	Constraint increase control of the user over data
	Can add constraint at the time of creation of the table or at time of altering the table.
	Can be column level or table level
	Constraint names are given automatically If not specified
	Constraint name must ne unique in a schema
	
	5 types of constraints:
		Not Null:
			Prevents insertion of NULL values
			NOT NULL constraint can only be created at the column-level
			NOT NULL constraint are typically used in the column definition of the create table
			CONSTRAINT key word is used t specify name to the constraint and recommended to use.
			E.g.
			Column_name DATA_TYPE CONSTRAINT column_not_null NOT NULL
			
		Unique:
			No duplicate values allowed except null values
			For composite unique constraint the multiples columns can have null values.
			Composite unique constraint must result in unique row
			Composite constraint can contain max 32 columns
			CONSTRAINT name is optional but recommended to provide one.
			Composite unique keys can be created only at column level
			Oracle creates unique index implicitly on unique keys
			CONSTRAINT word is also optional
			Inserting null values allowed with columns having unique constraint
		Primary Key
			Oracle creates UNIQUE index when creating a primary key
			It can be a single column(simple PK) or combination(composite PK) of column which identifies the row of the column uniquely
			A primary key constraint is the combination of a NOT NULL and UNIQE constraint
			Only one primary key is allowed
			E.g.
			Manager_id NUMBER CoNSTRAINT PK_MANAGERS PRIMARY KEY <- column level
			CONSTRAINT PK_MANAGERS PRIMARY KEY (manager_id, column2) < -table level
		Foreign Key <- 
			The relationship is established between the parent tables primary/unique key and a column or a set of columns in the child table.
			ON DELETE CASCADE, ON DELETE SET NULL
			A referenced record must exist in parent table before you can insert a related record in child table.
			It’s a referential integrity constraint
			It can be column or set of columns user to enforce a relationship between a parent table and child table
			REFERENCES keyword is used to specify a column and table as foreign key.
			FOREIGN KEY keyword is used only when column  name is not present before CONSTRANT keyword
			Foreign key is added to the child table and parent has the original column
			E.g.
			Department_id NUMBER CONSTRAINT mgr_dept_id_fk REFERENCES departments(department_id)_, <- column level FK constraint
			CONSTRAINT mgr_emp_fk FOREIGN_KEY (manager_id) REFERENCES employees(employee_id) <- table level FK constraint
			
			Sample column can be primary and foreign key at the same time
			2 types of foreign keys:
				Simple <- one to one column
				composite foreign keys <- more than one columns in parent and child table. These have to be created at table level
			Entry in parent table must exist before adding to the child table.
			Same column can have 2 or more constraints
			
		Check constraint 
		
		Ensures a column or a group of columns meet a specific condition
	
	
